.TH EUCLID-CLI-EDS 1 "November 2026" "euclid-cli 2.3.2" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eds \- manage DNS zones and records, and ask the name server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B eds
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B eds
module is euclid's domain name service. Its DNS server listens on UDP
and TCP port 1053 and answers questions from three sources:
.IP \(bu 2
.B zones you store in it
\- managed with the actions below and answered authoritatively, the way
a hosted zone is;
.IP \(bu 2
.B the names euclid gives what it runs
\- every application, every live worker node and the manager have a
name under
.IR euclid.internal ,
rebuilt every few seconds from what is actually running (see
.B SERVICE NAMES
below);
.IP \(bu 2
.B upstream resolvers
\- every other name is forwarded to them, but only when some are
configured and only for clients on the networks allowed to recurse.
.PP
This command manages the zones and records over the API, with the usual
credentials and grants. The DNS port itself has no credentials: anybody
who can reach it can ask it a question. The server is described in
.BR euclid-eds (8).
.SH ZONES
A zone is a domain name \-
.I corp.example
\- and every record stored below it. Once a zone exists, the server
answers for every name in it: with the records stored, and with
NXDOMAIN for every other name.
.PP
.B A zone's name is unique across the installation,
not only within an account. Every other euclid resource is named per
account and namespace, but a DNS question carries no account, so two
accounts each holding
.I corp.example
would leave the server nothing to choose by. The account and namespace
say whose the zone is and who may change it. Its ERN,
.IR ern:eds:<region>:<account>:<namespace>:zone:<name> ,
is what a grant names. Another account's zone is answered 404, the same
as one that does not exist.
.PP
Each zone gets an
.B SOA
and an
.B NS
record of its own. The SOA serial is the time of the zone's last change
in seconds, so it moves with every write. The NS record names the
configured name server, unless NS records are stored at the apex.
.PP
Zones cannot be created in
.IR euclid.internal ,
which holds the service names.
.SH RECORDS
Records are written and removed as
.BR "record sets" :
every value of one type at one name.
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1)
replaces the whole set at once, so there is never a moment when half the
old addresses and half the new ones are served.
.BR euclid-cli-eds-update-record (1)
changes part of one \- its TTL, or single values added and removed \-
without resending the rest.
.PP
The types that can be stored are
.BR A ,
.BR AAAA ,
.BR CNAME ,
.BR MX ,
.BR NS ,
.BR PTR ,
.B SRV
and
.BR TXT .
Values are written as in a zone file, without the quoting \- see
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1).
.SH NAMES
Wherever a record's name is given, it can be:
.TP
.I api
relative to the zone \-
.IR api.corp.example ;
.TP
.I api.corp.example
fully qualified, because it already ends in the zone;
.TP
.I api.corp.example.
fully qualified, because of the trailing dot;
.TP
.IR @ " or nothing"
the zone's apex itself;
.TP
.I *.dev
a wildcard, answering for every name below
.I dev.corp.example
that has no records of its own.
.PP
Names are compared without regard to case. An answer carries the name
the way the client spelled it.
.SH SERVICE NAMES
.TP
.I manager.euclid.internal
A/AAAA \- the manager.
.TP
.I <node>.node.euclid.internal
A/AAAA \- each worker node whose lease is live.
.TP
.I <application>.app.euclid.internal
A/AAAA \- every address a running instance of the application is at.
.TP
.I _http._tcp.<application>.app.euclid.internal
SRV \- each instance's port, with its node, or the manager, as the
target.
.PP
They are served with a TTL of five seconds, because the autoscaler
starts and stops instances far more often than a resolver would
otherwise look again. Names are lowercased and anything but a letter, a
digit or a hyphen becomes a hyphen:
.I Image_Processing
is
.IR image-processing .
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-zone
Creates a zone the name server answers for. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-create-zone (1).
.TP
.B get-zone
Shows a zone, its serial and how many record sets it holds. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-get-zone (1).
.TP
.B list-zones
Lists the account's zones. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-list-zones (1).
.TP
.B update-zone
Changes a zone's TTL or description. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-update-zone (1).
.TP
.B delete-zone
Deletes a zone and every record in it. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-delete-zone (1).
.TP
.B put-record
Writes the records of one type at one name, replacing any there. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-put-record (1).
.TP
.B update-record
Changes a record set's TTL, or adds and removes single values. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-update-record (1).
.TP
.B list-records
Lists a zone's record sets. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-list-records (1).
.TP
.B delete-record
Removes the records of one type at one name. See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-delete-record (1).
.TP
.B get-answer
Shows what the name server answers for a name, without a DNS client.
See
.BR euclid-cli-eds-get-answer (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I eds
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B eds
commands are authenticated using the session created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1),
loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials ,
and signed with RFC 9421 HTTP Message Signatures using the stored
access key if there is one.
.SH LIMITS
No zone transfers (AXFR/IXFR) \- a secondary that wants the zones reads
them through the API. No DNSSEC signing. No CAA, NAPTR or other record
types beyond the eight above. Only one instance of the server can run,
since two cannot share the port.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, the request could not be sent, or the server
rejected it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
A zone for an office network, an API behind two addresses, and the
web site as an alias of it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eds create-zone --name corp.example
euclid-cli eds put-record --zone corp.example --name api --type A \e
    --value 10.1.2.3 --value 10.1.2.4
euclid-cli eds put-record --zone corp.example --name www --type CNAME \e
    --value api.corp.example
euclid-cli eds get-answer --name www.corp.example
.fi
.RE
.PP
Asking the server directly, and finding an application's instances:
.PP
.RS
.nf
dig @127.0.0.1 -p 1053 www.corp.example
dig @127.0.0.1 -p 1053 image-processing.app.euclid.internal
dig @127.0.0.1 -p 1053 _http._tcp.image-processing.app.euclid.internal SRV
.fi
.RE
